--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Result
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

id_major = 0x0102 is TAIKO_MAJOR exactly (include/linux/mfd/wcd9xxx/core.h:
TAIKO_MAJOR = cpu_to_le16(0x102)). The part is a WCD9320, identified from
its own identification registers rather than inferred from its enumeration
address and board wiring.

id_minor = 0x0001. The downstream wcd9xxx_codecs table carries two Taiko
entries, TAIKO_MAJOR with minor 0x0 and with minor 0x1, so 0x1 is a valid
Taiko variant; downstream treats it as the later revision. Recorded as read
rather than relabelled.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

- The previous all-zero read is now fully explained. The only change was
  adding WCD9XXX_REGISTER_START_OFFSET (0x800): the register map begins
  0x800 into the SLIMbus value-element space, so chip id is at 0x804..0x807
  and version at 0x808. Reading raw 0x04..0x08 hit an unimplemented region.
- The chip version element at 0x808 reads 0x00, whereas
  WCD9XXX_A_CHIP_VERSION__POR in the downstream header is 0x20. Downstream
  identifies parts by id_major/id_minor and does not use this register for
  that purpose, so it is recorded as an observation, not a discrepancy that
  affects identification.
- The bytes also differ from the header's power-on-reset defaults
  (0,0,0,1), which would decode to major 0x0100. The silicon reports its
  real identity rather than those generic defaults.
- The logical address was 0xcb here and in the previous session, and 0x00 in
  the first. It is assigned by the manager and is not stable across boots.
- Still no regmap, DAI, mixer, DAPM, MBHC or sound card. Nothing was played
  or captured.
